::ENG4_U01_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Digital tools can expand learning when students use them with purpose. A note-taking application can help a student organize source ideas, and a shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. However, technology does not automatically improve learning. What is the passage's central claim? {
~A. Digital tools always improve learning.#This ignores the qualification.
=B. Digital tools can help learning when students use them purposefully.#Correct. This captures the qualified central claim.
~C. Digital tools should never be used in school.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Peer feedback is never useful.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. What does evaluate mean? {
~A. Ignore#This is the opposite.
~B. Copy exactly#This is not evaluation.
~C. Avoid completely#This is not evaluation.
=D. Judge or assess#Correct. Students must judge support.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: If students copy information without paraphrasing, accept suggestions without evaluating them, or use digital tools to avoid careful thinking, the tool weakens rather than strengthens academic work. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Digital tools weaken work only when students use them to avoid thinking carefully.#Correct. This preserves the condition.
~B. Digital tools always weaken academic work.#This removes the condition.
~C. Students should accept every digital suggestion.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Copying without paraphrasing strengthens academic work.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Technology does not automatically improve learning. If students copy information without paraphrasing or accept suggestions without evaluating them, the tool weakens academic work. Which oral explanation is strongest? {
~A. The passage is about technology and learning things.#This is vague.
=B. The passage argues that tools help only when students keep responsibility for paraphrasing and evaluating information.#Correct. This is coherent and accurate.
~C. Technology helps only with apps and documents.#This is too narrow.
~D. The passage says feedback should be ignored.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which sentence has correct subject-verb agreement? {
=A. Responsible technology use requires careful thinking.#Correct. Subject and verb agree.
~B. Responsible technology use require careful thinking.#The singular subject needs requires.
~C. Responsible technology uses requires careful thinking.#This creates a subject-verb error.
~D. Responsible technologies use requires careful thinking.#This is unclear and nonstandard.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. What does efficient mean in this context? {
~A. Completely private#This is unrelated.
=B. Productive with less wasted time#Correct. Efficient feedback uses time well.
~C. Incorrect#This is unrelated.
~D. Decorative#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Digital tools can expand learning when students use them with purpose. Which paraphrase is too broad? {
~A. Digital tools can support learning when used purposefully.#This is accurate.
~B. Purposeful technology use can expand learning.#This is accurate.
~C. Digital tools may expand learning when students use them intentionally.#This is accurate.
=D. Technology is always good for every learner.#Correct. This is too broad and unsupported.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which presentation order is most coherent? {
=A. Claim, paraphrased evidence, rationale, conclusion#Correct. This order is logical.
~B. Conclusion, unrelated opinion, source meaning, claim#This is not coherent.
~C. Vocabulary list, personal story, random quote, claim#This is not focused.
~D. Evidence, evidence, evidence, no explanation#This lacks rationale.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A note-taking application can help a student organize source ideas, and a shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. Which evidence supports the claim that tools can help learning? {
~A. Students may copy information without paraphrasing.#This supports the warning, not the benefit.
~B. Technology does not automatically improve learning.#This is the qualification.
=C. A note-taking application can help organize source ideas.#Correct. This supports the benefit.
~D. Tools can weaken work.#This supports the warning.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which word best describes the passage's standard for technology use? {
=A. Responsible#Correct. The passage uses responsible to describe appropriate use.
~B. Automatic#The passage rejects automatic improvement.
~C. Decorative#This is unrelated.
~D. Careless#This is the opposite.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A note-taking application can help a student organize source ideas, and a shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. Which sentence is punctuated correctly? {
~A. A note-taking application can help, a student organize source ideas.#The comma is misplaced.
~B. A note-taking application, can help a student organize source ideas.#The comma separates subject and verb.
~C. A note-taking application can, help a student organize source ideas.#The comma interrupts the verb phrase.
=D. A note-taking application can help a student organize source ideas.#Correct. No comma interrupts the verb phrase.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: However, technology does not automatically improve learning. What does this sentence do in the passage? {
~A. It proves technology is useless.#This overstates the sentence.
~B. It gives a definition of peer feedback.#This is not a definition.
=C. It qualifies the earlier positive examples.#Correct. It limits the claim.
~D. It introduces a new unrelated topic.#It is directly related.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: If students copy information without paraphrasing, accept suggestions without evaluating them, or use digital tools to avoid careful thinking, the tool weakens rather than strengthens academic work. What does paraphrasing mean? {
~A. Copying word for word#This is the opposite of paraphrasing.
=B. Restating meaning accurately in one's own words#Correct. This is paraphrasing.
~C. Ignoring the source#This is not paraphrasing.
~D. Replacing reasoning with technology#This is not paraphrasing.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which oral sentence best uses academic voice? {
~A. Tech is cool if you do stuff right.#This is informal and vague.
~B. Apps are just awesome for school.#This is informal and unsupported.
~C. Technology is a thing that does learning.#This is unclear.
=D. Responsible technology use requires students to preserve meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly.#Correct. This is precise and academic.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. However, technology does not automatically improve learning. Which paraphrase preserves both ideas? {
~A. Shared documents always guarantee better learning.#This overstates the passage.
=B. Shared documents may improve feedback efficiency, but technology still requires purposeful use.#Correct. This preserves benefit and qualification.
~C. Technology cannot help feedback.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Peer feedback is unrelated to technology.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which sentence correctly capitalizes the idea? {
~A. responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning.#The first word should be capitalized.
~B. Responsible Technology Use requires Students to preserve Source Meaning.#Unnecessary words are capitalized.
=C. Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning.#Correct. The sentence begins with a capital letter.
~D. responsible Technology use requires students to preserve source meaning.#Capitalization is inconsistent.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: If students copy information without paraphrasing, accept suggestions without evaluating them, or use digital tools to avoid careful thinking, the tool weakens rather than strengthens academic work. What does weakens mean? {
~A. Makes stronger#This is the opposite.
~B. Organizes carefully#This is unrelated.
~C. Preserves meaning#This is unrelated.
=D. Reduces strength or effectiveness#Correct. Weakens means makes less effective.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which response best synthesizes the idea? {
~A. Technology is always responsible.#This is unsupported.
~B. Technology replaces source meaning.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. Students use technology responsibly when they keep meaning accurate, judge support, and explain conclusions clearly.#Correct. This integrates the three required actions.
~D. Students should communicate without evidence.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Digital tools can expand learning when students use them with purpose. Which sentence would be an effective opening claim for an oral explanation? {
=A. Digital tools can strengthen learning, but only when students use them purposefully.#Correct. This is focused and qualified.
~B. My topic is digital stuff.#This is vague.
~C. Technology is always good.#This overstates the claim.
~D. I like note-taking apps.#This is personal preference.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: If students copy information without paraphrasing, accept suggestions without evaluating them, or use digital tools to avoid careful thinking, the tool weakens rather than strengthens academic work. Which sentence uses commas correctly in the series? {
~A. Students should paraphrase evaluate and think carefully.#Commas are missing.
=B. Students should paraphrase, evaluate, and think carefully.#Correct. The series is clear.
~C. Students, should paraphrase, evaluate and think carefully.#The first comma is incorrect.
~D. Students should paraphrase, evaluate and, think carefully.#The last comma is misplaced.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A note-taking application can help a student organize source ideas, and a shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Note-taking apps and shared documents can support organization and feedback.#Correct. This preserves the examples.
~B. Note-taking apps prevent all source problems.#This overstates the passage.
~C. Shared documents make feedback unnecessary.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Digital tools remove the need for students to think.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which academic word best names "source meaning, support, and conclusions" working together in a response? {
~A. Decoration#This is unrelated.
~B. Isolation#This is the opposite.
=C. Integration#Correct. Integration combines parts into a connected whole.
~D. Fragment#This is incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: However, technology does not automatically improve learning. Which oral explanation best explains the word however? {
~A. However signals that the passage is adding an unrelated point.#This is not correct.
~B. However proves technology is always harmful.#This overstates the contrast.
~C. However means the same thing as therefore.#This is incorrect.
=D. However signals a contrast between possible benefits and a warning.#Correct. It marks the qualification.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: If students copy information without paraphrasing, accept suggestions without evaluating them, or use digital tools to avoid careful thinking, the tool weakens rather than strengthens academic work. Which claim is best supported by this evidence? {
~A. Tools always weaken academic work.#This is too broad.
~B. Copying without paraphrasing improves learning.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. Tools can weaken academic work when students use them to avoid careful thinking.#Correct. This preserves the evidence and condition.
~D. Suggestions should always be accepted.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. Which word best describes a conclusion that the audience can follow? {
=A. Clear#Correct. A clear conclusion is easy to follow.
~B. Vague#This is the opposite of clear.
~C. Hidden#This is not effective communication.
~D. Automatic#This is unrelated.
}
